    "summary": "Somalia's Foreign Ministry condemned an attack on a power station supplying electricity to the Prophet's Mosque in Medina, Saudi Arabia, calling it a violation of international law.",
    "content": "Mogadishu — The Federal Government of Somalia has strongly condemned an attack on a power facility that provides electricity to the Prophet's Mosque in Medina, Saudi Arabia. The incident occurred on 3 October 2026, according to a statement from Somalia's Ministry of Foreign Affairs and International Cooperation. The ministry described the target as civilian infrastructure serving one of Islam's most sacred sites.\n\nThe statement characterized the attack as an affront to the sanctity of holy places and a breach of international law, as well as a violation of Saudi Arabia's sovereignty. Somalia expressed its full solidarity with the Kingdom of Saudi Arabia in the wake of the incident. The ministry did not provide further details on the nature of the attack or those responsible.\n\nThe Prophet's Mosque is the second holiest site in Islam and hosts millions of pilgrims annually. Attacks on civilian infrastructure in the vicinity of such sites are rare and typically draw broad international condemnation. Saudi authorities have not yet issued a public response to the Somali statement.\n\nSomalia's condemnation reflects its longstanding diplomatic and religious ties with Saudi Arabia, which has been a key partner in humanitarian and development assistance. The Saudi kingdom also hosts a significant Somali diaspora, many of whom reside in cities like Jeddah, Riyadh, and Medina.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, the attack carries particular resonance given the community's deep religious and cultural connections to the holy cities. Many Somalis travel to Saudi Arabia for pilgrimage, and the safety of these sites is a matter of widespread concern. The incident may heighten anxieties among diaspora members planning travel or with relatives in the region.\n\nSomali community leaders abroad have previously voiced alarm over any threat to Islamic holy sites, viewing such acts as attacks on shared heritage. The Somali government's prompt condemnation underscores its alignment with Saudi Arabia on security and religious matters.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/10/03/soomaaliya-oo-cambaareysay-weerarka-lagu-qaaday-xarun-koronto-oo-adeegta-masjidka-nabiga/",
