    "id": "news-1790960621066-2",
    "title": "Fires in Somaliland Leave 238 Families Homeless in Three Months",
    "original_title": "House fires cause huge losses for hundreds of families in Hargeisa",
    "slug": "fires-in-somaliland-leave-238-families-homeless-in-three-months",
    "summary": "The Fire and Rescue Department reports that 238 families in Hargeisa and other parts of Somaliland have lost their homes to fires in the past three months, with many unable to rebuild.",
    "content": "The Fire and Rescue Department in Somaliland has reported that 238 families across Hargeisa and other regions have been left homeless after fires destroyed their homes over the past three months. The figures, released this week, highlight a recurring problem in the region where many homes are built with flammable materials such as corrugated iron and wood.\n\nOne affected family is that of Fosiya Abdi Adan, who lived in a two-room corrugated iron house in the Dhego-sayn neighbourhood of Hargeisa. In July, a fire broke out while she was away working as a house cleaner. She returned at around 2:30pm to find her home in flames. Fosiya, who supports a family of 10, said they could not afford to rebuild and have since been moving between relatives' homes for shelter.\n\nThe cause of the fires has not been specified, but local authorities note that rapid construction and the use of inexpensive building materials contribute to the high risk. The Fire and Rescue Department has been working to respond to incidents but faces challenges in reaching affected areas quickly.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, the news is a reminder of the vulnerabilities faced by relatives back home. Many in the diaspora send remittances to support family members, and such disasters often prompt appeals for urgent assistance. The loss of homes can push already struggling families deeper into poverty, and the diaspora community frequently plays a key role in providing emergency relief and rebuilding efforts.\n\nThis incident underscores the need for improved fire safety measures and affordable housing in Somaliland. As families like Fosiya's continue to cope with the aftermath, the support of relatives abroad remains crucial. The Fire and Rescue Department has not announced any specific plans to address the situation, but the scale of the loss has drawn attention to the broader issue of housing resilience in the region.\n\nRead the original report from Radio Ergo here: https://radioergo.org/en/2026/10/house-fires-cause-huge-losses-for-hundreds-of-families-in-hargeisa/",
    "source": "radioergo.org",
