    "summary": "A Saudi-led coalition reports a Houthi drone strike on a power station supplying electricity to the Prophet's Mosque in Medina, marking an expansion of the conflict to new areas.",
    "content": "The Saudi-led coalition supporting Yemen's internationally recognized government announced that a drone attack targeted a power station in Medina, Saudi Arabia. According to coalition officials, the strike damaged a power conversion unit but did not cause a widespread outage in the city's electricity network. The facility is responsible for supplying power to the Prophet's Mosque, one of Islam's holiest sites.\n\nThe coalition stated that an investigation of the drone debris indicated the attack was carried out by the Houthi movement. The Houthis, who control large parts of northern Yemen, have not yet commented on the allegation. This incident follows years of cross-border attacks by the Houthis on Saudi infrastructure, including airports and oil facilities, which the coalition has often intercepted.\n\nThe conflict in Yemen has been ongoing since 2014, when the Houthis took over the capital, Sanaa, prompting a Saudi-led military intervention in 2015. The war has created one of the world's worst humanitarian crises, with millions of Yemenis displaced and dependent on aid. The targeting of Medina, a city of profound religious significance, marks a notable escalation in the conflict.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, many of whom have family and business ties in both Saudi Arabia and Yemen, this development raises concerns about regional stability. Saudi Arabia hosts a large Somali expatriate community, and any disruption to its infrastructure or security could have direct repercussions. Additionally, the proximity of the conflict to Somalia's own security challenges underscores the interconnected nature of instability in the Horn of Africa and the Arabian Peninsula.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/10/02/xiisadda-sacuudiga-iyo-xuutiyiinta-oo-gaartay-madiina-khamis-mushait-iyo-cadan/",
