    "content": "Three explosions were heard in different areas of Addis Ababa, Ethiopia's capital, according to an intelligence official and diplomatic sources. The blasts occurred as fighting continues to spread in the northern part of the country. No official cause has been determined, and investigations are still underway.\n\nOne of the explosions was heard near the headquarters of the Ethiopian National Defense Force, while another was reported in a different part of the city. The sources, who spoke on condition of anonymity, did not specify whether the explosions are connected to the ongoing conflict in the north. Ethiopian authorities have not issued a statement regarding the incidents.\n\nThe explosions come amid a deteriorating security situation in northern Ethiopia, where government forces have been battling armed groups for months. The conflict has displaced thousands and raised concerns about stability in the region. Addis Ababa has largely been spared from the violence, but recent incidents have heightened tensions.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, many of whom have family and business ties in Ethiopia, the explosions raise concerns about safety and potential disruptions to trade routes. Ethiopia hosts a significant Somali population and serves as a key transit point for goods and people. Any escalation in urban violence could affect diaspora remittances and travel plans.\n\nEthiopian officials have not commented on the explosions, and it remains unclear whether they are linked to the northern conflict. The government has previously accused opposition groups of carrying out attacks in urban areas, while opposition figures have denied involvement. The situation remains fluid.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/10/02/qaraxyo-ka-dhacay-addis-ababa-iyo-mucaaradka-oo-lagu-eedeeyay-weerarro-drone-ah/",
